This module describes how to implement a network socket in Mint.

A concrete socket implementation should inherit {Network.Socket} and provide
standard lifecycle methods that the higher-level channel and protocol classes
expect. The implementation owns the low-level handle and updates the socket
state as operations progress.

> [!WARNING]
> The constructor of a socket implementation must call the base constructor
> before any other operation.
>
> ```mn
> class MySocketClass : Network.Socket {
>     const def new(self, ...) {
>         if self = Network.Socket.new(self) {
>             // initialize the low-level handle and state
>             return self
>         }
>     }
> }
> ```

A typical implementation provides the following methods:

- `connect` and `finalizeConnection` to establish or complete a client connection.
- `read` and `write` to exchange data as instances of {Serializer.DataStream}.
- `bind`, `listen`, and `accept` to prepare the socket as a server and accept incoming peers.
- `shutdown` and `close` to shut the socket down cleanly.
- `getHandle`, `isNonBlocking`, and `setNonBlocking` to expose the low-level handle and readiness behavior.

The implementation should also update the socket state with {Network.Socket.State}
and raise {Exception.SocketError} on failures so higher-level wrappers such as
{Network.Channel}, {Network.AsyncChannel}, and {Network.EventChannel} can react
appropriately to the result.
